WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.200
He fights for money, for honor,&nbsp;
for…child custody?! There's more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.640
to the best movies of Jean-Claude&nbsp;
Van Damme's career than you think.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:14.480
By 1993, Jean-Claude Van Damme was a bona fide&nbsp;
action star. That was the year he starred in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:21.040
Nowhere to Run as dangerous antihero Sam Gillen.&nbsp;
A hard-luck small-time criminal, he's on a prison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.240
transfer bus when it crashes, allowing him to&nbsp;
escape. He isn't about to go back to serving time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.760
behind bars, but he also has no interest in a life&nbsp;
of crime. So he lives on the fringes of a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:37.280
community before being discovered by a beautiful&nbsp;
young woman named Clydie and her young children.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:41.840
When a group of corrupt land developers tries&nbsp;
to push Clydie and her family off their land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:47.200
Sam steps in and plays hero. Now at the center of&nbsp;
a high-stakes tug of war and with cops closing in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:52.000
Sam has to stop pulling his punches if he's&nbsp;
going to stay a free man and help his newfound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:58.450
friends. A breezy, heartfelt adventure, Nowhere&nbsp;
to Run is just the right mix of action and drama.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:04.480
2018's The Bouncer is a dark character study of&nbsp;
a beleaguered man named Lukas who settles for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.600
job as a strip club bouncer to support his young&nbsp;
daughter after fleeing a murder charge and facing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.880
jail time. Filmed in Van Damme's native French,&nbsp;
this thriller follows Lukas as he struggles to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:20.400
scrape by on the verge of poverty, torn between&nbsp;
a life of crime and his duty as an informant.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:26.000
The Bouncer scored positive marks from critics,&nbsp;
who praised Van Damme's noirish turn in a dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.840
moody film that doesn't ask him to&nbsp;
wink to the camera. French director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:33.040
Julien Leclercq was able to get&nbsp;
a compelling performance out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.589
a star whose late career has mostly&nbsp;
been marked by direct-to-video duds.

00:01:37.589 --> 00:01:42.800
In 1991, Jean-Claude Van Damme and&nbsp;
director Sheldon Lettich reunited for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.280
twice the action in Double Impact. Van&nbsp;
Damme stars as a pair of twin brothers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.600
Chad and Alex, separated as infants when&nbsp;
their wealthy parents were gunned down in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:57.120
a vicious attack by hitmen in Hong Kong in the&nbsp;
1960s. Chad is taken by the family bodyguard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.560
while Alex is left at an orphanage&nbsp;
in China by their loyal maid.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.000
Cut ahead to 1991 when Chad is lured to Hong Kong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.480
where he meets Alex, who has reunited them to&nbsp;
get revenge on the man who killed their parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:12.400
and get their hands on their share of their&nbsp;
inheritance. But their plan puts them in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.080
the path of dangerous gangsters,&nbsp;
drug lords, and violent assassins.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.040
"I don't believe this. My brother?"

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:26.160
Double Impact gives Van Damme the opportunity to&nbsp;
punch out, take down, and kick butt twice as hard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.560
The reviews, however, were half as kind,&nbsp;
with few critics anticipating the action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:35.360
classic this would become. At least Roger&nbsp;
Ebert, in a shrugging two-star review,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:40.240
noted the film's high production values&nbsp;
and exotic setting, as he also declared,

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:43.760
"[Van Damme] seems thoroughly&nbsp;
comfortable with his feet above his head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.573
a position many martial arts&nbsp;
experts often find themselves in."

00:02:47.573 --> 00:02:54.000
The 1994 sci-fi action adventure Timecop&nbsp;
threw Van Damme into the future world of 2004,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.560
where time travel has become a reality.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, criminals have begun using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.400
it to alter the past, forcing the creation&nbsp;
of the Time Enforcement Commission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.840
tasked with traveling back in time and catching&nbsp;
future criminals. Van Damme stars as Max Walker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:14.560
and appears in two time periods, as a police&nbsp;
officer in 1994 and a TEC agent in 2004.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:19.760
In 1994, Walker is the target of mysterious&nbsp;
assassins in a deadly bombing that leaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:25.280
his wife dead. A decade later, he learns that&nbsp;
a powerful senator is using time travel as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:30.160
part of a conspiracy to attain power. It's up&nbsp;
to Walker alone to set things right. But he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.960
also given the chance to save his wife, even if&nbsp;
it means potential consequences for the future.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:39.680
A thrilling joyride, Timecop is a&nbsp;
deft mix of patented Van Damme action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:45.840
and mind-bending plotting. It was the biggest hit&nbsp;
of his heyday, grossing $102 million worldwide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.080
It wasn't exactly well-reviewed&nbsp;
at the time, but over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:56.716
it's become a sci-fi cult classic, spawning a&nbsp;
TV spin-off and a 2003 direct-to-DVD sequel.

00:03:56.716 --> 00:04:02.800
Universal Soldier likely had little ambition to&nbsp;
become a classic when it was released in 1992.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:07.360
It was hardly a big-money blockbuster, but&nbsp;
with Van Damme and co-star Dolph Lundgren,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:11.520
it had a pair of action stars to fill&nbsp;
out its compelling high-concept premise.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:17.200
During the Vietnam War, U.S. Army soldier Luc&nbsp;
Deveraux discovers that one of his comrades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:21.840
Sergeant Andrew Scott, has been brutalizing&nbsp;
innocent civilians. They're both killed in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.800
deadly confrontation, only to be resurrected&nbsp;
decades later in a secret government project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.680
called the Universal Soldier Program. With&nbsp;
cybernetic implants and bio-modifications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.400
they become unstoppable mindless killing machines.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:39.680
An all-time sci-fi cult classic despite&nbsp;
its less-than-stellar initial reception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.720
Universal Soldier kicked off a multipart saga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.897
including numerous sequels, as well as a&nbsp;
line of comic books, video games, and more.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.528
"I just want to eat."

00:04:49.794 --> 00:04:55.760
In 2010, Sylvester Stallone assembled a team of&nbsp;
veteran action heroes to create The Expendables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:00.800
an over-the-top action flick unlike any other.&nbsp;
It starred Stallone alongside the likes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:07.280
Jason Statham, Jet Li, Terry Crews, and Dolph&nbsp;
Lundgren. 2012's Expendables 2 added the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.040
missing piece from its predecessor,&nbsp;
the Muscles from Brussels himself.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:15.840
In the film, the titular team of veteran&nbsp;
mercenaries go up against Van Damme as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:20.800
the movie's main villain, the suitably named&nbsp;
Jean Vilain, who leads his own gang of rival&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:26.080
mercenaries called the Sangs. They're both after&nbsp;
the same thing: a computer data bank that holds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:31.280
the location of five tons of enriched plutonium&nbsp;
left over after the Cold War by the Soviet Union.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:35.600
Van Damme goes to extraordinary scenery-chewing&nbsp;
heights here, including one scene where he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:40.080
kicks a bowie knife into Liam Hemsworth's&nbsp;
chest. The film actually received decent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:44.541
reviews and became the highest-grossing&nbsp;
non-animated film of Van Damme's career.

00:05:44.541 --> 00:05:49.920
Sudden Death was released in 1995, at the peak&nbsp;
of Van Damme's star power. He takes on the role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:54.880
of Darren McCord, a French-Canadian firefighter&nbsp;
serving in Pittsburgh. But after failing to save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.680
a family from a house fire, he's been relegated&nbsp;
to a security job at the local sports stadium.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:04.080
During the NHL Stanley Cup Finals, with the&nbsp;
Pittsburgh Penguins squaring off against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:09.360
Chicago Blackhawks, terrorists seize control of&nbsp;
the arena. With the Vice President in attendance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:12.560
the terrorists take a number&nbsp;
of high-profile hostages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:17.600
and threaten to blow up the arena that they've&nbsp;
wired with powerful explosives. It now rests on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:21.920
the shoulders of the downtrodden McCord to&nbsp;
stop a madman and save the vice president.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:26.480
"This is the most powerful&nbsp;
plastic explosive in the world."

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:31.360
Sudden Death got mixed reviews&nbsp;
but won over plenty of fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:36.320
including a rave from the Los Angeles Times.&nbsp;
It also features the best Van Damme fight ever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:40.754
in which he battles a penguin mascot to&nbsp;
the death and utterly destroys a kitchen.

00:06:40.754 --> 00:06:46.400
The first American film for Hong Kong director&nbsp;
John Woo, the guns-blazing action flick Hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:51.120
Target is definitely a favorite for many a&nbsp;
Jean-Claude Van Damme fan. In this outing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:56.080
he plays Chance Boudreaux, a homeless former&nbsp;
Marine in New Orleans. After saving a young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:00.960
woman named Natasha from a group of muggers on&nbsp;
the street, he's hired to be her protection on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.440
mission to locate her missing father, a Vietnam&nbsp;
veteran who's been living on the streets. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:10.240
the investigation unfolds, they uncover a&nbsp;
scheme run by a group of sinister tycoons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:14.880
who have been hunting the homeless for sport and&nbsp;
who set Natasha and Chance as their next targets.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:19.760
Reviews at the time were mixed, but the film is&nbsp;
packed with arguably the best action of any of Van&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:25.040
Damme's films thanks to Woo's characteristically&nbsp;
over-the-top touch. Nowadays, Hard Target is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:29.680
seen as a landmark in the history of the genre,&nbsp;
thanks in particular to Van Damme pulling off some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:35.120
amazing stunts, like standing on a motorcycle&nbsp;
while firing a gun at an oncoming vehicle.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:36.640
"I've been looking all over for you."

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:39.800
"You've been looking in the wrong places."

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:45.680
1990's Lionheart sees Van Damme competing in&nbsp;
street-level tournaments in the inner city.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:50.400
He stars as Lyon Gaultier, whose fighting&nbsp;
nickname is "Lionheart." To earn money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:54.960
to travel westward and see his brother, he&nbsp;
enters an illegal underground street fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:59.440
joining a fledgling circuit of champions&nbsp;
that caters to the wealthiest spectators.

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:03.920
Like his 1980s output, Lionheart wasn't&nbsp;
exactly well-received by critics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:07.920
But the film gives Van Damme some real&nbsp;
drama to chew on as well as some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:12.480
best action of his early career. As&nbsp;
Brian Orndorf of Blu-ray.com noted,

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:16.880
"The fight scenes are the highlights of Lionheart,&nbsp;
allowing Van Damme to do what he does best,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:21.025
trying to make believe that his opponents&nbsp;
have a shot at dropping Lyon to his knees."

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:26.160
A year after 1988's Bloodsport made him a&nbsp;
martial arts star, Van Damme returned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:31.680
the ring for Kickboxer, another no-holds-barred&nbsp;
tournament fighter film. His unrelenting on-screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:36.240
fights are kicked up a notch, and the film&nbsp;
boasts an even more powerful personal story.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:41.680
Van Damme plays Kurt Sloane, fighting assistant&nbsp;
to his brother Eric, the American kickboxing world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:46.480
champion. Having conquered the American circuit&nbsp;
more than once, Eric has grown bored and heads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:51.760
to Thailand to compete against a more brutal breed&nbsp;
of competitors. He's set to face off against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:57.920
vicious Tong Po, a massive mountain of a man who's&nbsp;
merciless in the ring. Against Kurt's objections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:03.200
Eric fights the unstoppable Po and is so&nbsp;
savagely beaten that he's left paralyzed.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:07.120
Desperate for vengeance, Kurt finds a&nbsp;
wise Muay Thai master who trains him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:11.920
in the art of kickboxing and sends him&nbsp;
on a quest to defeat Tong Po. The film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:17.680
blood-soaked climactic final match is one for&nbsp;
the ages. It also spawned a series of sequels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:21.760
though Van Damme wouldn't return until&nbsp;
the franchise was rebooted with Kickboxer:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:26.975
Vengeance in 2016. And of course, we&nbsp;
can't forget that iconic dance scene.

00:09:26.975 --> 00:09:30.320
"Can you still dance like that?&nbsp;
You still got those dance moves?"

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:31.638
"I still got the move."

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:34.560
The movie that made Jean-Claude Van Damme a star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:38.880
Bloodsport followed in the footsteps of&nbsp;
fighting films like Rocky and The Karate Kid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:42.800
But Bloodsport featured a more intense&nbsp;
and more graphic form of fighting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:48.320
as Van Damme got the chance to truly show off what&nbsp;
a talent he was on screen. Admittedly, the plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:53.200
is rife with cliches, but that doesn't matter&nbsp;
when our leading man is in peak physical form.

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:57.040
Inspired by a supposedly true story&nbsp;
that wound up being completely phony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:02.560
the film stars Van Damme as Frank Dux, an&nbsp;
American Army captain who competes in the Kumite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:07.840
a brutally violent Hong Kong fighting tournament.&nbsp;
He makes an enemy in the form of Kumite champion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:12.480
Chong Li, whom he'll have to defeat to&nbsp;
win it all. Bloodsport was an instant hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:15.379
and it remains a top dog in the Van Damme canon.

00:10:15.379 --> 00:10:20.560
Unlike any film that he's made&nbsp;
before or since, the crime drama JCVD&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:26.160
brought serious self-awareness as Van Damme&nbsp;
turned to more serious fare. The year was 2008,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:31.040
and he was nearly a decade removed from headlining&nbsp;
a major Hollywood film, having starred in a series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:36.400
of direct-to-DVD B-movies since then. But he still&nbsp;
had a lot left in the tank, and so he delivered a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:41.840
powerful performance that earned him rave reviews&nbsp;
as an exaggerated, downtrodden version of himself.

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:46.720
The plot finds Van Damme struggling to find any&nbsp;
work at all, let alone the kinds of action roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:51.680
from his glory days. On top of that, after&nbsp;
a bitter court battle, he loses custody of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:56.880
his daughter. Just as he's reached wits' end, he&nbsp;
gets entangled in a bank robbery gone wrong, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:01.680
police believing him to be one of the thieves.&nbsp;
While at first it's a case of mistaken identity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:06.000
Van Damme seizes on the opportunity to get&nbsp;
money for himself. As the media gets wind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:10.400
that the Hollywood hero has turned crook,&nbsp;
the situation quickly escalates. Giving the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:15.840
action star's career an existential kick, with a&nbsp;
nearly 7-minute monologue about his public image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:20.560
JCVD serves as a reminder of the&nbsp;
power that one's own legacy can hold.

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:28.492
"Stupid to kill people. They're so&nbsp;
beautiful. So today I pray to God."

